Description: Export the request of an inhibition only once the backend has handled the call.
 xdg-desktop-portal answered Inhibit and exported its request at once, and called the backend afterwards, from a
 thread, once it had looked up the application's permissions. A Close of the request which arrived in between was
 forwarded to the backend before the backend had created its request, and failed. The backend then still asked
 gnome-session for the inhibition, which stayed until the application disconnected from the bus. GTK closes the
 request as soon as an application uninhibits, so an application which inhibits and uninhibits right away, as Text
 Editor does while it loads a file, left an inhibitor behind, which GNOME Shell lists in its logout dialog. An
 application which inhibits when the session is about to end, and calls QueryEndResponse once Inhibit returned, also
 often had the backend receive QueryEndResponse first, so the session could end without the inhibition
 (https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/issues/1881).
 .
 The handler, which runs in a thread of its own, now calls the backend synchronously, and exports the request and
 answers only once the backend has returned, which it does after it has exported its own request. In the tests, the
 mock backend, when it expects a Close, now returns from Inhibit and keeps its request until the request is closed, as
 xdg-desktop-portal-gtk does, instead of returning only on Close. A new test closes a request right after Inhibit
 returned, while the mock backend takes 200 ms to handle the call, and another one calls QueryEndResponse right after
 Inhibit returned and checks that the backend receives Inhibit first.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/issues/854
Forwarded: https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/pull/2199
Last-Update: 2026-11-11
